body:
`bulkCreate` currently inserts raw `data` and returns the database response without applying the same ID normalization and SQLite JSON/boolean serialization used by `create()` (`formatInput`/`formatOutput`). Under the `IDataDriver` contract, bulkCreate should return records with `id` set and without leaking driver-specific formats; consider generating missing IDs per row and formatting input/output consistently (or delegating to `create()` / a shared helper).
```suggestion

    // Normalize IDs and apply driver-specific input formatting per row.
    const ids: Array<string | number> = [];
    const rowsToInsert = data.map((row) => {
      const { _id, ...rest } = row || {};
      const toInsert: Record<string, any> = { ...rest };

      if (_id !== undefined && toInsert.id === undefined) {
        toInsert.id = _id;
      } else if (toInsert.id === undefined) {
        toInsert.id = nanoid(DEFAULT_ID_LENGTH);
      }

      ids.push(toInsert.id);

      const formatted = this.formatInput(object, toInsert);

      if (this.tablesWithTimestamps.has(object)) {
        if (this.isSqlite) {
          const now = new Date().toISOString().replace('T', ' ').replace('Z', '');
          formatted.created_at = now;
          formatted.updated_at = now;
        } else {
          const nowFn = this.knex.fn.now();
          formatted.created_at = nowFn;
          formatted.updated_at = nowFn;
        }
      }

      return formatted;
    });

    let inserted: any[];
    if (this.isSqlite) {
      // SQLite does not reliably support .returning('*') on INSERT.
      await builder.insert(rowsToInsert);
      inserted = await this.getBuilder(object, options).whereIn('id', ids).select('*');
    } else {
      inserted = await builder.insert(rowsToInsert).returning('*');
    }

    return inserted.map((row) => this.formatOutput(object, row) || row);
```

`updateMany` updates with the raw `data` object, but `update()` applies `formatInput()` (SQLite JSON serialization) and timestamp handling. For consistency and correctness (especially on SQLite JSON fields), `updateMany` should format/sanitize the update payload the same way as `update()` before calling `builder.update()`.
```suggestion
    const formattedData = this.formatInput(object, data, options);
    const count = await builder.update(formattedData);
```
